standfirst: "Find the lowest ROAS your Google Ads can run at without losing money, the target ROAS that leaves the profit you want, and what every level of ROAS actually earns you after costs."
title: "Break-even ROAS Calculator for Google Ads | DigitalFadi"
description: "Free break-even ROAS calculator. Factor in product cost, delivery, fees, returns and VAT to find your minimum ROAS and a profitable target ROAS."
faqs:
  - q: "What is break-even ROAS?"
    a: "Break-even ROAS is the return on ad spend at which the profit from a sale exactly covers the cost of the ads that produced it. Below it, every sale loses money. The formula is 1 divided by your profit margin before ad costs, so a 25 percent margin gives a break-even ROAS of 4, or 400 percent in Google Ads."
  - q: "How do I calculate break-even ROAS?"
    a: "Take the average order value, subtract product cost, delivery, payment fees, returns and any other cost per order to get the profit before advertising. Divide the order value by that profit. If an order is worth 100 and leaves 30 before ads, the break-even ROAS is 100 divided by 30, which is 3.33."
  - q: "What target ROAS should I set in Google Ads?"
    a: "Set it above break-even by enough to leave the profit you need. Google Ads takes target ROAS as a percentage, so a target of 5 is entered as 500 percent. Start near your recent actual ROAS rather than jumping straight to a much higher figure, because a target far above current results can sharply cut volume while the bidding adjusts."
  - q: "Does VAT affect my ROAS?"
    a: "It can. If your conversion tracking reports the price including VAT or sales tax, Google Ads counts revenue you never keep. Tick the VAT option in this calculator and it removes the tax before working out profit, so the break-even figure matches what your account actually reports."
  - q: "Why is my ROAS good but the business is not making money?"
    a: "ROAS measures revenue, not profit. A ROAS of 3 sounds healthy, but with a 25 percent margin the break-even point is 4, so every sale loses money. The table in this calculator shows the profit or loss at each ROAS level for your own costs."
  - q: "Is the information I enter saved?"
    a: "Yes, in this browser only, so your figures are still there next time on the same device. Nothing is uploaded or sent anywhere, including to me. The Clear button removes them."
---
